 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 532040,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "<figure>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/fb786f4afccdd5ab.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n(credit: modification of work by William J. Ripple, et al./<i>Biological Conservation</i>)\n</figure>",
   "stem_html": "<p>In 1920s, wolves were killed to extinction in the Yellowstone Park. This resulted in an overpopulation of elk in 1980s. In 1990, park rangers reintroduced wolves. This graph shows the population of various species in the park after the wolves were reintroduced to the ecosystem.</p>\n<p>Make a claim about willow, elk and wolf populations based on this information.</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1040956,
     "content_html": "Willow populations are negatively affected by elk populations if there are no wolves in the area."
    },
    {
     "id": 1040957,
     "content_html": "Willow populations are not affected by elk or wolf populations"
    },
    {
     "id": 1040958,
     "content_html": "Willow populations are positively affected by elk populations if there are no wolves in the area."
    },
    {
     "id": 1040959,
     "content_html": "There is not enough information to make a claim in this study."
    }
   ],
